## Omit — drop keys from an object type

```ts
interface User {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  passwordHash: string;
}

type PublicUser = Omit<User, 'passwordHash'>;
// { id: string; name: string }
```

`Omit<T, K>` produces `T` without the listed keys. It accepts a union to drop several:

```ts
type Minimal = Omit<User, 'passwordHash' | 'id'>;
```

## Pick — the mirror

`Pick<T, K>` keeps only the listed keys — useful when you want a few rather than all-but-a-few:

```ts
type Credentials = Pick<User, 'id' | 'passwordHash'>;
```

## Exclude — for unions, not objects

`Omit` works on object keys. To remove members from a union type, use `Exclude`:

```ts
type Status = 'draft' | 'published' | 'archived';
type Live = Exclude<Status, 'draft'>; // 'published' | 'archived'
```

`Omit` is itself built from `Pick` and `Exclude`.

## Why derive instead of redefine

The point is a single source of truth. A `PublicUser` derived from `User` cannot drift — add a field to `User` and `PublicUser` updates automatically (you only revisit it if the new field is sensitive). Re-declaring a separate interface means two definitions to keep in sync by hand.

## A caveat

`Omit` is key-based, not deep — it does not recurse into nested objects. For component props, pairing it with the base prop type keeps every variant honest: each component derives from one base, so a change to the base propagates everywhere.

