## Marking a deliberate override

`override` on a method declares "this replaces a method of the same name on the base class":

```ts
class Logger {
  log(msg: string) { console.log(msg); }
}

class TimestampLogger extends Logger {
  override log(msg: string) {
    super.log(`[${Date.now()}] ${msg}`);
  }
}
```

## noImplicitOverride — where it earns its keep

On its own `override` is mostly documentation. With `noImplicitOverride: true` in tsconfig (this project sets it), the compiler enforces two rules:

**1. Every override must be marked.** A method that shadows a base method without `override` is an error — so overrides are always visible at a glance.

**2. Every `override` must really override something.** If the base method does not exist, `override` is an error:

```ts
class TimestampLogger extends Logger {
  override logg(msg: string) {} // Error — Logger has no 'logg'
}
```

## The bug it prevents

Rule 2 is the real value. Without it, if someone renames `Logger.log` to `Logger.write`, the subclass's `log` silently stops overriding anything and becomes a brand-new, never-called method — a subtle, runtime-only bug. With `noImplicitOverride`, that rename breaks the build immediately and points you straight at the stale subclass.

## Compile-time only

`override` emits nothing. It is a checking aid, like `readonly` or `private` — pure intent the compiler verifies, then erases.

